<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout>
  <title>Section 3.5: Properties and Applications of Logarithms</title>
  <p>
    <alert>MTH 124</alert>
  </p>

<p workspace="1in">
  <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
    <p><md>b = 10^a ~~~ \iff ~~~ \log_{10}(<fillin characters = "1"/>) = <fillin characters="1"/></md>
<md>\log_{10}(10^t) = <fillin characters = "1"/></md>
<md>10^{\log_{10}(y)} = <fillin characters = "1"/></md></p>
    <p><md>b = e^a ~~~ \iff ~~~ \ln(<fillin characters = "1"/>) = <fillin characters = "1"/></md>
<md>\ln(e^t) = <fillin characters = "1"/></md>
<md>e^{\ln(y)} = <fillin characters = "1"/></md></p>
  </sidebyside>
  </p>

  <assemblage>
    <title>Property 1</title>
    <p>Multiplication inside logs.</p>
  </assemblage>
<p workspace="0.1in"/>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Property 2</title>
    <p>Division inside logs.</p>
  </assemblage>
<p workspace="0.1in"/>

  <assemblage>
    <title>Property 3</title>
    <p>Powers inside logs.</p>
  </assemblage>

  <p workspace="0.15in">* Use these properties to solve any exponential function, regardless of base!</p>
  <example>
    <sidebyside>
      <p workspace="1in">
        <md>4^t = 7</md>
      </p>
      <p workspace="1in">
        <md>7\cdot 3^{2t}+4 = 10</md>
      </p>
    </sidebyside>
  </example>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 3.5.2</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Solve each of the following equations <term>exactly</term> and then find an estimate that is accurate to 5 decimal places.</p>
      <p>
        <ol marker="a." cols="2">
          <li>
            <p workspace="1in"><m>3^t = 5</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p workspace="1in"><m>4\cdot 2^t-2=3</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p workspace="1in"><m>3.7\cdot(0.9)^{0.3t}+1.5 = 2.1</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
              <p workspace="1in"><m>72 - 30(0.7)^{0.05t}=60</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p workspace="1in"><m>\ln(t)=-2</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
              <p workspace="1in"><m>3+2\log_{10}(t)=3.5</m></p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      </statement>

  </activity>


<assemblage>
    <title>The graph of <m>y=\ln(x)</m></title>
        <p>Graph <m>e^x</m> is shown above the line <m>y=x</m>, and <m>\ln(x)</m> is shown below the line <m>y=x</m>. Left is zoomed in, right is zoomed out.</p>

    <p>
  <sidebyside widths="28% 28% 40%">
    <image>
      <shortdescription>
        The graph e^x and ln(x) on the same plot.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-3-5a">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="7">
          <definition>f(t)=e^t</definition>
          <definition>g(t)=ln(t)</definition>
          <definition>h(t)=t</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-3,-3,3,3)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes" />
            <graph function="f"/>
            <graph function="g" dash="4 4" stroke="red"/>
            <graph function="h" dash="10 10" stroke="black"/>
            <label anchor="(0.5,2.8)"><m>y=e^x</m></label>
            <label anchor="(1,-2.7)"><m>y=\ln(x)</m></label>
            <point p="(-1,f(-1))" alignment="w"><m>(-1,e^{-1})</m></point>
            <point p="(e^(-1),g(e^(-1)))" alignment="e"><m>(e^{-1},-1)</m></point>
            <point p="(0,f(0))" alignment="w"><m>(0,1)</m></point>
            <point p="(1,g(1))" alignment="e"><m>(1,0)</m></point>
            <point p="(1,f(1))" alignment="e"><m>(1,e)</m></point>
            <point p="(e^(1),g(e^(1)))" alignment="nw"><m>(e,1)</m></point>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    <image>
      <shortdescription>
        The graph e^x and ln(x) on the same plot.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-3-5b">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="7">
          <definition>f(t)=e^t</definition>
          <definition>g(t)=ln(t)</definition>
          <definition>h(t)=t</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-15,-15,15,15)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes" />
            <graph function="f"/>
            <graph function="g" dash="4 4" stroke="red"/>
            <graph function="h" dash="10 10" stroke="black"/>
            <label anchor="(5.5,13.8)"><m>y=e^x</m></label>
            <label anchor="(12,3.7)"><m>y=\ln(x)</m></label>

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    <p>The graph of <m>y=\ln(x)</m>:
      <ul>
        <li><p>vertical asymptote at <m>x=0</m></p></li>
        <li><p>passes through <m>(1,0)</m></p></li>
        <li><p>is always increasing</p></li>
        <li><p>is always concave down</p></li>
        <li><p>increases without bound</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </sidebyside>

 </p>

    
  </assemblage>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 3.5.3</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>In the questions that follow, we compare and contrast the properties and behaviors of exponential and logarithmic functions.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0cm">
      <statement>
        <p>Go to Desmos and plot <m>f(x)=-e^{-(x-1)}+1</m>
 and <m>g(x) = \ln(x)</m>.
 Compare the properties of the two, noting what they have in common and what they don't.</p>
        <tabular>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>\quad f(x)=-e^{-(x-1)}+1 \quad </m></cell>
            <cell><m>\quad \quad g(x)=\ln(x) \quad \quad </m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">Domain:</cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">Range:</cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"><m>x</m>-intercept</cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"><m>y</m>-intercept</cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">increasing or decreasing:</cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">concave up or down:</cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">grow to <m>\infty</m> or bounded:</cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0cm">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>h(t)=-ae^{-k(t-b)}+c</m>,
 where <m>a</m>,
 <m>b</m>,
 <m>c</m>,
 and <m>k</m>
 are positive constants. Describe <m>h</m>
 as a transformation of the function <m>y=e^{t}</m>.  (How does <m>c</m> transform <m>e^{t}</m>, ..., think shifts and stretches vertically or horizontally. Can use Desmos to help you)</p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>a</m>:</p></li>
            <li><p><m>b</m>:</p></li>
            <li><p><m>c</m>:</p></li>
            <li><p><m>k</m>:</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>r(t) = a\ln(t-b)+c</m>,
 where <m>a</m>,
 <m>b</m>,
 and <m>c</m>
 are positive constants. Describe <m>r</m>
 as a transformation of the function <m>y=\ln(t)</m>.  (Can use Desmos to help you.)</p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>a</m>:</p></li>
            <li><p><m>b</m>:</p></li>
            <li><p><m>c</m>:</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Data for the height of a tree is given in the table below; time 
 is measured in years and height of the tree is given in feet. At <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/0yy">gvsu.edu/s/0yy</url>, you can find a Desmos worksheet with this data already input.</p>
        <tabular>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell  right="minor">t</cell>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell>5</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>7</cell>
            <cell>8</cell>
            <cell>9</cell>
            <cell>10</cell>
            <cell>11</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">h(t)</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>9.5</cell>
            <cell>13</cell>
            <cell>15</cell>
            <cell>16.5</cell>
            <cell>17.5</cell>
            <cell>18.5</cell>
            <cell>19</cell>
            <cell>19.5</cell>
            <cell>19.7</cell>
            <cell>19.8</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>Do you think this data is better modeled by a logarithmic function of form  <m>{p(t)=a\ln(t-b)+c}</m>
 or by an exponential function of form <m>{q(t)=me^{-rt}+n}</m>.
 Provide reasons based in how the data appears and how you think a tree grows, as well as by experimenting with sliders appropriately in Desmos. (Note: you may need to adjust the upper and lower bounds of several of the sliders in order to match the data well.)</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 3.5.4</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Solve each of the following equations for the exact value of the unknown variable (<m>k</m>).</p>
    <p>
      <ol marker="a." cols="2">
        <li>
          <p workspace="1.5in"><m>41 = 50e^{-7k}</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p workspace="1.5in"><m>65=34+47e^{-45k}</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p workspace="1.5in"><m>7e^{2k-1}+4=32</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p workspace="1.5in"><m>\frac{5}{1+2e^{-10k}}=4</m></p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    </statement>
 
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <title>Logs in the <q>wild.</q></title>
    <introduction>
      <p>When a population grows (decays) at a rate proportional to the current population, the population follows the model of exponential growth (decay): <md>P(t)=Ae^{kt}</md> where <m>P(t)</m> is the population at time <m>t</m>, <m>A</m> and <m>k</m> are constants.  If <m>k&gt;0</m> we are talking about growth (<m>P</m> is increasing), if <m>k&lt;0</m> we are talking about decay (<m>P</m> is decreasing).  Many populations grow/decay exponentially; one of the most common is bacteria.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Consider a population of bacteria <m>P(t)</m> where <m>P</m> is measure in millions of cells and <m>t</m> is in hours.  We know <m>P(0)=2.475</m>, and <m>P(10)=4.298</m>.  Find a model of the form <m>P(t)=Ae^{kt}</m> that fits this data by first plugging in <m>P(0)</m> to determine <m>A</m>, and then plugging in <m>P(10)</m> to determine <m>k</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>How many bacteria are there after 20 hours (<m>t=20</m>)?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>How long does it take the population of bacteria to reach 1 billion cells (1000 million cells)?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

</handout>
